At the junction of NH 44 and NH 544 near Salem, the corridor veers southwest into Tamil Nadu, where the highway winds past the historic town of Coimbatore. Here, the road’s median‑divided lanes and frequent rest stops (the Coimbatore Service Area and the Sathyamangalam Eco‑Park) allow drivers to refuel and recharge. Continuing along NH 544, the route threads through the lush tea gardens of Wayanad before turning onto NH 766, the lifeline that plunges into the Western Ghats and emerges at Palakkad. From Palakkad, the final stretch to Kozhikode is a scenic 80‑km descent through the Malabar coast, punctuated by the Velliyur and Kottakkal rest stops that offer local delicacies and a chance to stretch legs.

Travel time averages 14 to 16 hours, depending on weather and traffic. The corridor’s design—wide, well‑lit, and with frequent service bays—ensures minimal delays. Shree Ashirwad’s fleet of GPS‑tracked trucks exploits this infrastructure to deliver luggage with pinpoint precision. Real‑time routing algorithms adjust for any congestion near Bangalore or Coimbatore, rerouting through alternate lanes on NH 544 or diverting onto the newly upgraded NH 766 bypass. The GPS system also monitors driver fatigue, automatically scheduling mandatory 15‑minute breaks at designated rest stops, thereby guaranteeing both safety and punctuality.

